    "create_time": 1791265726.407,
    "update_time": 1791265730.1440687,
    "content": {
     "content_type": "text",
     "parts": [
      "1. **\"Did students learn more?\"** We didn't measure learning outcomes. We measured whether TAs found stuck students sooner, which is a precondition, and a learning study is the obvious next step.\n2. **\"Isn't this surveillance?\"** It can be, which is why it was opt-in, pseudonymous and explicitly not for grading. The risk is real, and our Discussion argues deployments need student-visible timelines and short retention.\n3. **\"Why should I trust your labels?\"** Three raters, κ = 0.71 computed before adjudication, with the guide released. The weakest boundary is stalled-after-error vs. productive, and we report that.\n4. **\"Does it generalize?\"** Only partly. Unretrained on a second offering, macro-F1 dropped from 0.72 to 0.66, so course-specific features matter.\n5. **\"Why not just use the LLM's chat logs?\"** Many stuck students never ask. Telemetry catches the silent ones, and that's the population TAs miss.\n6. **\"Six TAs is tiny.\"** Yes. It's a formative study about workflow, not an effect-size claim, and we frame it that way.\n7. **\"Couldn't a simple idle timer do this?\"** Idle time alone confuses reading and thinking with being stuck. The error context is what separates stalled-after-error from a pause, and it carries most of the F1.\n8. **\"What would you do differently?\"** Recruit a second course before building the classifier, and involve students in deciding what TAs get to see."
     ]
    },
